---
/**
 * MediaSplitBlock — the `media_split` layout: a photograph on one side, copy on
 * the other.
 *
 * ONE COMPONENT, TWO BANDS. The pilot page uses this shape twice — `#what` and
 * `#natural` — and they are not the same markup. `#what` is
 * `.what-grid > .what-img + .what-text`; `#natural` is
 * `.natural-grid > .natural-text + .natural-img`. Both class families exist on
 * every sibling sheet, so the difference could not be papered over by picking
 * one name; and neither could be hardcoded, because a component that only knew
 * `.what-*` would render the candidacy band as a landscape 5:4 photo on the
 * wrong side of the page.
 *
 * WHAT ACTUALLY DIFFERS, AND WHY IT IS ONE PROP RATHER THAN SIX.
 *
 * Measured across the six detail-page sheets that carry both (porcelain-veneers,
 * teeth-whitening, smile-makeover, clear-aligners, and the two they were lifted
 * from), the two families differ in exactly five ways and all five move
 * together:
 *
 *              .what-*                     .natural-*
 *   DOM order  media first                 text first
 *   columns    1fr 1fr                     1fr 1.1fr
 *   photo      aspect-ratio 5/4            aspect-ratio 4/5
 *   heading    margin-bottom 26px          margin-bottom 28px
 *   quote      never present               `.natural-quote` above the prose
 *
 * There is no page anywhere in src/pages that mixes them — no `.what-grid` with
 * the image second, no `.natural-img` in landscape. The families are one design
 * decision seen from two sides, and the decision is which side the photo is on.
 * So `media_side` selects the family, and the other four follow from it. The
 * pilot page's own comment says the same thing from the content end: the `#what`
 * photo is "Landscape source, which suits this 5:4 slot; the 4:5 portraits do
 * not."
 *
 * `ratio` is then a genuine second axis, and it is expressed as a departure
 * from whatever the family's own split already is: `even` is what `.what-grid`
 * ships, `wide-media` is what `.natural-grid` ships, so the pilot's two bands
 * need no modifier class at all and the HTML diff stays empty. A modifier is
 * emitted only when the editor asks for a split the family does not already
 * have.
 *
 * THE SECOND BUTTON — A GAP THAT IS NOW CLOSED, AND WHY IT IS A FIELD PAIR.
 * The `#what` band ships TWO buttons — "Free Virtual Consult" to `#consult` and
 * "How It Works" to `#process`. This block used to have one `cta_label` /
 * `cta_href` pair, so backfilling that band lost the second button. The fix is
 * `cta_label_2` / `cta_href_2` on the layout, read here as `ctaLabel2` /
 * `ctaHref2`, and it had to be editable rather than written in: hardcoding a
 * secondary button would put "How It Works" on every media_split band on the
 * site, including the eleven that have no process section to point it at.
 *
 * Contrast the `.inline-cta` pair in ProcessStepsBlock, which IS written in.
 * The distinction is whether the button is site data or page content: those two
 * are "Book Online" and "Call {phone}" on all eight bands and come from
 * src/data/contact.ts, so a per-page field for them is a per-page chance to
 * publish the wrong phone number. These two are neither — the destination is a
 * different anchor on nearly every page that carries them — so they are fields.
 *
 * `body_2` — A SECOND PARAGRAPH THAT CARRIES MARKUP, RENDERED WITH `set:html`.
 * The `.prose` column is one `<p>` on the pilot and two on teeth-whitening's
 * `#natural`, where the second ends "...better results with
 * <a class="vs-link" href="/cosmetic-dentistry/porcelain-veneers/">porcelain
 * veneers</a>." FaqBlock had to WEIGH escaped against permissive, because its
 * answers rendered fine either way on the pilot and the decision was about the
 * other six pages. There is no comparable weighing here: the entire reason this
 * field exists is copy the escaped path cannot render, and escaping it would
 * print the anchor tag as visible text at the patient. So `set:html`, and the
 * same trust boundary FaqBlock names applies — the practice's own editors, not
 * public input, so knowing which pages carry tags is the control, not
 * sanitizing.
 *
 * The `<p>` wrapper is emitted here rather than expected in the value, because
 * the markup being reproduced is a second `<p>` INSIDE `.prose` and the field
 * is a plain textarea. A wysiwyg would arrive pre-wrapped, and `<p><p>…</p></p>`
 * is invalid nesting that the parser closes early — leaving a stray empty `<p>`
 * in the DOM and a broken gap on screen. `body2IsBlock` below detects that and
 * passes the value through unwrapped instead.
 *
 * `checklist` — THE `.candidate-list`, DECLARED SINCE THE FIELD LANDED AND
 * DRAWN BY NOTHING UNTIL NOW.
 * The layout has always carried a list-of-lines repeater and this component
 * never rendered it, so any band backfilled from a page that ships one lost the
 * whole list silently — four numbered points, gone, with the band still looking
 * finished. The shape is on four of the eight pages left to migrate:
 * `.candidate-list` on bone-grafting, all-on-4-single-arch and
 * full-mouth-dental-implants, and the identical thing under the name
 * `.tech-list` on sinus-lift. The five rules behind the two names are the same
 * declarations character for character in all four sheets, so this draws ONE
 * class, `.candidate-list`, and sinus-lift gets an additive alias in its own
 * sheet when that page migrates. Two class names for one shape is how a rule
 * gets edited in one place and not the other.
 *
 * THE MARKER IS DERIVED, NOT STORED. `01`, `02`, `03` is the position of the
 * line in the list, so it is computed from the index here and is deliberately
 * NOT a field. Had it been one, deleting the second of four rows would leave
 * `01 03 04` on the page until a human noticed, and reordering rows in wp-admin
 * — the one edit a repeater exists to make cheap — would scramble it every
 * time.
 *
 * `lead` is the bolded opening of a line and it already includes the em dash it
 * ends with, because the dash belongs to the bold run in the markup being
 * reproduced (`<b>Unpreserved extraction sites —</b> Patients whose…`).
 * It is optional: every list the site ships outside these four pages is plain
 * lines, and a blank `lead` must produce no `<b>` AND no leading space inside
 * the `<span>` — a space there is visible, because `.candidate-list li` is a
 * grid and the text column starts exactly where the span does.
 *
 * NOTE FOR WHOEVER RUNS THE NEXT PHASE: `checklist` is not in
 * blockSelectionSet() yet (manifest.ts says it "goes in when the component that
 * needs it does"). Until that one line is added the prop arrives `undefined`
 * and this draws nothing, which is exactly the pre-existing behaviour — but it
 * also means this markup is inert until the fragment asks for
 * `checklist { lead item }`.
 *
 * Styles are co-located per docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 2.5, modelled on
 * FinalBand.astro: no page namespace, `--vs-*` tokens directly, band as a
 * prop, self-owned padding, zero margin.
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import { phoneHref } from "../data/contact";

/** The layout's image sub-field, as wpgraphql-acf returns an ACF image. */
export interface MediaSplitImage {
  node?: {
    sourceUrl?: string | null;
    altText?: string | null;
    mediaDetails?: { width?: number | null; height?: number | null } | null;
  } | null;
}

/**
 * One line of the layout's `checklist` repeater — GraphQL type
 * `PageFieldsBlocksChecklist`. Both sub-fields are optional at the schema level
 * (ACF returns `null` for a row an editor left blank), so neither can be typed
 * as a plain string here without lying to every caller.
 */
export interface MediaSplitPoint {
  /** The bolded opening, em dash included. Blank draws no `<b>` at all. */
  lead?: string | null;
  /** The line itself. */
  item?: string | null;
}

/**
 * One card of the layout's `sub_cards` repeater — GraphQL type
 * `PageFieldsBlocksSubCards`.
 *
 * `{ tag, title, body }` is read off the MARKUP of the two bands this closes,
 * not off their class names: `.config-card` on
 * all-on-4-single-arch.astro:427-431 is `<span class="tag">` + `<h4>` + `<p>`,
 * and `.cause-card` on sinus-lift.astro:396-399 is the same triple with NO `<p>`
 * at all — its data has no `body` key to render (sinus-lift.astro:63-68).
 *
 * So `body` is optional, and a blank one must draw no `<p>` rather than an empty
 * one. An empty paragraph in a `display: flex` card is not a collapsed nothing:
 * the card's `gap` applies between flex items whether or not they have content,
 * so it is a visible 10px strip under every title in a four-across grid.
 *
 * Every sub-field is optional at the schema level for the reason
 * `MediaSplitPoint` gives — ACF returns `null` for a row an editor left blank.
 */
export interface MediaSplitSubCard {
  /** The small uppercase kicker above the title. */
  tag?: string | null;
  /** The card's `<h4>`. */
  title?: string | null;
  /** The card's paragraph. Absent on all four sinus-lift cards by design. */
  body?: string | null;
}

interface Props {
  /** DOM id, already deduplicated by PageBlocks.astro. */
  id?: string;
  /** The same value as `id`. Accepted so the spread in PageBlocks is lossless. */
  anchor?: string | null;
  /** Rail label. Read by the page that builds the rail, not by this band. */
  navLabel?: string | null;
  band?: string | null;
  eyebrow?: string | null;
  /** May contain `<em>`, and nothing else (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md, Fact 3). */
  heading?: string | null;
  body?: string | null;
  /**
   * A second paragraph in the same `.prose` column, rendered with `set:html`.
   * Carries inline markup — `<a class="vs-link">`, `<em>`, `<strong>`. See the
   * header for why this one is permissive and FaqBlock's answer had to argue
   * for it. Empty renders no paragraph at all.
   */
  body2?: string | null;
  /**
   * The `.veneers-callout` aside that sits after `.prose` on teeth-whitening's
   * `#natural` band (teeth-whitening.astro:554-570) — its `<h3>`.
   *
   * A field pair rather than a page-specific band, on exactly the terms
   * comparison_cards got `.safety-callout`: both are a boxed aside of the same
   * shape inside a band, and giving one a field and not the other is how a page
   * comes back ninety words short with nobody able to say which element went.
   * Blank on both pages that render this block today, and blank draws no aside.
   */
  calloutHeading?: string | null;
  /**
   * The aside's prose. Two paragraphs in the copy this reproduces, the second
   * ending in an `<a class="vs-link">`, so it is HTML-bearing and blank-line
   * split the same way comparison_cards does it.
   */
  calloutBody?: string | null;
  /**
   * "2" | "3" | "4" — the track count of the secondary grid below the split.
   *
   * NULL IS A REAL VALUE HERE, not a missing one. ACF writes a select's default
   * only when an editor ADDS a row, so all eleven bands back-filled before this
   * field existed return `null`. The fallback below is therefore 2 — what
   * `.aos4 .config-grid` ships (all-on-4-single-arch.css:371-372), i.e. the
   * shape a band that predates the select already had — and not "unset".
   */
  subColumns?: string | null;
  /**
   * The `.what-sub` card grid that sits BELOW the split, inside the same
   * `.wrap`: `.config-grid` with two cards on all-on-4-single-arch
   * (all-on-4-single-arch.astro:413-434) and `.cause-grid` with four on
   * sinus-lift (sinus-lift.astro:380-402).
   *
   * ONE CLASS FOR ONE SHAPE, on the rule the header already argues for
   * `.candidate-list`: `.config-card` and `.cause-card` are the same article
   * with different padding, and drawing both names would be two places to edit
   * one rule. `.config-card` is the name that survives, because it is also what
   * the three `.alternatives-grid` bands call their cards.
   *
   * An empty or absent repeater draws NO `.what-sub` AT ALL — not an empty one.
   * The wrapper carries `margin-top: 70px`, so an empty `<div class="what-sub">`
   * is 70px of visible dead space at the foot of all eleven bands nobody edited.
   */
  subCards?: (MediaSplitSubCard | null)[] | null;
  image?: MediaSplitImage | null;
  /** Overrides the media item's own alt text when the editor filled it in. */
  imageAlt?: string | null;
  /** "left" | "right" — selects the class family. See the header. */
  mediaSide?: string | null;
  /** "even" | "wide-text" | "wide-media" */
  ratio?: string | null;
  quote?: string | null;
  /**
   * The numbered `.candidate-list` under the pull quote. See the header for why
   * the marker is not in here and why `lead` carries its own dash. An empty or
   * absent list draws no `<ul>` — which is every band saved to date, since the
   * field has never been queried.
   */
  checklist?: (MediaSplitPoint | null)[] | null;
  ctaLabel?: string | null;
  ctaHref?: string | null;
  /** Secondary button. Empty label renders no second button. */
  ctaLabel2?: string | null;
  ctaHref2?: string | null;
}

/**
 * band value -> the modifier class the page's markup already uses.
 *
 * `paper` maps to nothing because the pilot's paper bands are a bare
 * `class="section"`. `alt` and `dark` are inherited names and inherited
 * liabilities — `.section.alt` is charcoal on porcelain-veneers.css and cream
 * on teeth-whitening.css (Fact 4) — which is why the colour below is written
 * against the band VALUE and the class is only the hook the un-migrated page
 * sheet still needs.
 */
const BAND_CLASS: Record<string, string | undefined> = {
  paper: undefined,
  cream: "cream",
  "sage-pale": "dark",
  sage: "sage",
  charcoal: "alt",
};

/**
 * Hover labels, by destination.
 *
 * `hoverLabel` renders as a `data-hover` attribute and as the button's second
 * label span, so it is part of the markup this block has to reproduce — and
 * there is no CMS field for it. Deriving it from the destination is not a
 * guess: `href="#consult"` is paired with "Get a Video" in 52 of the 54 places
 * that label appears across src/pages, and the phone and booking links are
 * unanimous. An unknown destination yields `undefined`, which Button.astro
 * already treats as "reuse the visible label".
 */
const CTA_HOVER: Record<string, string> = {
  "#consult": "Get a Video",
  "#process": "View Steps",
  "#faq": "See Answers",
  [phoneHref]: "Tap to Dial",
};

const {
  id,
  band = "paper",
  eyebrow,
  heading,
  body,
  body2,
  calloutHeading,
  calloutBody,
  subColumns,
  subCards,
  image,
  imageAlt,
  mediaSide = "left",
  ratio = "even",
  quote,
  checklist,
  ctaLabel,
  ctaHref,
  ctaLabel2,
  ctaHref2,
} = Astro.props;

const bandKey = typeof band === "string" && band !== "" ? band : "paper";
const bandClass = BAND_CLASS[bandKey];

/**
 * The eyebrow carries `light` on the two bands the page spells `.section.dark`,
 * and only there — the pilot's two media splits are charcoal and carry a plain
 * eyebrow. Inert under the current cascade, but it is in the HTML.
 */
const eyebrowLight = bandKey === "sage-pale" || bandKey === "sage";

/** Solid button on a dark band has to be the light variant, not the sage one. */
const isDarkBand = bandKey === "charcoal" || bandKey === "sage";

/** The class family, and with it the column split, the photo shape and the order. */
const mediaRight = mediaSide === "right";
const fam = mediaRight ? "natural" : "what";

/**
 * A ratio modifier is emitted only when the editor asked for a split the family
 * does not already have. `.what-grid` is even; `.natural-grid` is wide-media.
 * Emitting nothing in those two cases is what keeps `class="what-grid"` and
 * `class="natural-grid"` the exact strings the page ships today.
 */
const familyRatio = mediaRight ? "wide-media" : "even";
const ratioKey = typeof ratio === "string" && ratio !== "" ? ratio : familyRatio;
const ratioClass = ratioKey === familyRatio ? undefined : `ratio-${ratioKey}`;

/**
 * The picture, or nothing.
 *
 * `<Image>` refuses a remote source without explicit intrinsic dimensions, so a
 * media item WordPress recorded no size for cannot be rendered at all — an SVG,
 * or an upload that did not finish. That has to degrade to a one-column band
 * rather than fail a build an editor triggered (the same rule
 * src/loaders/blog.ts and UnknownBlock.astro run on), so the media element is
 * simply omitted and the omission is reported to the build log where an
 * engineer will see it.
 */
const node = image?.node ?? null;
const src = typeof node?.sourceUrl === "string" ? node.sourceUrl : "";
const width = node?.mediaDetails?.width ?? 0;
const height = node?.mediaDetails?.height ?? 0;
const hasMedia = src !== "" && width > 0 && height > 0;

if (!hasMedia) {
  console.warn(
    `[blocks] media_split on ${Astro.url.pathname}${id ? `#${id}` : ""} has no usable picture — rendering the copy alone`,
  );
}

/** Editor override first, then the alt text stored on the file itself. */
const alt = (imageAlt ?? "").trim() || (node?.altText ?? "").trim() || "";

/**
 * The second paragraph, and whether it already carries its own block wrapper.
 *
 * The field is a textarea, so the expected value is an inline fragment and the
 * `<p>` below is this component's. The guard is for the day someone converts it
 * to a wysiwyg in wp-admin: ACF's `wpautop` would return `<p>…</p>`, nesting it
 * would produce invalid markup, and the parser's recovery leaves a stray empty
 * `<p>` in the band — which is precisely the class of diff this phase fails on.
 * Passing an already-wrapped value straight into `.prose` keeps the element
 * count right either way.
 */
const body2Html = typeof body2 === "string" ? body2.trim() : "";
const hasBody2 = body2Html !== "";
const body2IsBlock = /^<(p|div|ul|ol|blockquote)[\s>]/i.test(body2Html);

/* The aside, on the same terms as `body_2` above and as
   ComparisonCardsBlock.astro's `.safety-callout`. Blank-line split, because the
   copy is two paragraphs and `.veneers-callout p + p` is what spaces them —
   splitting on a bare newline instead would turn every soft wrap into a
   paragraph break. No `aria-labelledby` here, unlike the compare band: the
   markup being reproduced does not carry one, and adding it would be a
   difference the harness reports. */
const calloutHeadingText =
  typeof calloutHeading === "string" ? calloutHeading.trim() : "";
const calloutBodyHtml = typeof calloutBody === "string" ? calloutBody.trim() : "";
const calloutBodyIsBlock = /^<(p|div|ul|ol|blockquote)[\s>]/i.test(calloutBodyHtml);
const calloutParagraphs = calloutBodyIsBlock
  ? []
  : calloutBodyHtml
      .split(/\n\s*\n/)
      .map((part) => part.trim())
      .filter((part) => part !== "");
const hasCallout = calloutHeadingText !== "" || calloutBodyHtml !== "";

/**
 * The secondary grid's cards, on exactly the terms `checklistPoints` below
 * spells out: `Array.isArray` first, because an unqueried repeater arrives
 * `undefined` and a repeater an editor never opened arrives `null`, and `.map`
 * on either throws during the build of whatever page the band sits on.
 *
 * The filter drops a row only when EVERY sub-field is blank — the empty row ACF
 * leaves behind when someone clicks "Add row" and changes their mind. It is
 * deliberately not "a card needs a title": a card saved with only a tag is a
 * card an editor meant to save, and dropping it here would delete published copy
 * with no error anywhere to explain it.
 */
const subCardRows = (Array.isArray(subCards) ? subCards : [])
  .map((row) => ({
    tag: typeof row?.tag === "string" ? row.tag.trim() : "",
    title: typeof row?.title === "string" ? row.title.trim() : "",
    body: typeof row?.body === "string" ? row.body.trim() : "",
  }))
  .filter((card) => card.tag !== "" || card.title !== "" || card.body !== "");
const hasSubCards = subCardRows.length > 0;

/** "2" unless the editor picked otherwise. See the prop for why `null` has to
    land on a real track count rather than on "unset". */
const subColumnsKey = subColumns === "3" || subColumns === "4" ? subColumns : "2";

/**
 * THE CALLOUT BECOMES THE GRID'S SUB-HEAD, AND ONLY WHEN THERE IS A GRID.
 *
 * `calloutHeading` / `calloutBody` already carry the `<h3>` and the paragraph
 * that stand above these cards on both pages, so nothing new is mapped for them.
 * But this block renders that pair as `.veneers-callout` — a boxed aside INSIDE
 * the copy column — because a boxed aside is the only place the pair had ever
 * appeared. `.what-sub-head` is a centred head in the `.wrap`, a sibling of the
 * split, and the cards belong under it. Drawing the head in the copy column and
 * the cards below the split would leave "Upper Arch vs. Lower Arch: Which One Do
 * You Need?" boxed off in the right half of the band with two headless cards
 * floating beneath it — the band would look broken, not merely different.
 *
 * SWITCHED, NOT MOVED, and the switch is what keeps this additive: with no
 * `sub_cards` the pair is still the aside, byte for byte, which is every band
 * saved to date and every band that has ever rendered these two fields. A band
 * that fills BOTH is a shape no page in the corpus has; it gets the sub-head,
 * because that is the only reading in which the cards have a heading at all.
 */
const calloutIsSubHead = hasCallout && hasSubCards;

/**
 * The checklist lines, trimmed and emptied out.
 *
 * `Array.isArray` first because an unqueried repeater arrives `undefined` and a
 * repeater an editor never opened arrives `null`, and `.map` on either throws
 * during the build of whatever page the band happens to sit on.
 *
 * The filter drops rows with nothing in EITHER sub-field, and it runs BEFORE the
 * marker is computed. That ordering is the whole point: ACF leaves a blank row
 * behind whenever someone clicks "Add a point" and then changes their mind, and
 * numbering before the filter would spend `02` on a row that draws nothing and
 * print `01 03 04` under a list of three.
 *
 * The two branches are written on ONE template line below. Breaking `{point.item}`
 * onto its own line would put a newline-and-indent inside the `<span>` — a
 * leading space on the plain-line branch and a second space after `</b>` on the
 * other — and that span IS the text column of a two-column grid, so a leading
 * space is visible rather than collapsed against a preceding word.
 */
const checklistPoints = (Array.isArray(checklist) ? checklist : [])
  .map((row) => ({
    lead: typeof row?.lead === "string" ? row.lead.trim() : "",
    item: typeof row?.item === "string" ? row.item.trim() : "",
  }))
  .filter((point) => point.lead !== "" || point.item !== "");
const hasChecklist = checklistPoints.length > 0;

const hasCta = typeof ctaLabel === "string" && ctaLabel.trim() !== "";
const hasCta2 = typeof ctaLabel2 === "string" && ctaLabel2.trim() !== "";

/**
 * The row is drawn for EITHER button, not just the first.
 *
 * Gating the whole `.cta-row` on `ctaLabel` would mean a band that fills only
 * the second pair renders no buttons and no wrapper — silent content loss of
 * the kind that is invisible until someone reads the page. With `ctaLabel2`
 * empty this is identical to `hasCta`, so the two live bands are untouched.
 */
const hasCtaRow = hasCta || hasCta2;

/**
 * Secondary buttons are the ghost variant of whatever the primary is: sage
 * pairs with ghost on paper/cream, light pairs with ghost-light on the dark
 * bands. Both pairings are the site's own — `sage` + `ghost` on the paper
 * `#compare` band of teeth-whitening, `light` + `ghost-light` on the charcoal
 * `#what` band of clear-aligners, which is the band this reproduces.
 */
const primaryVariant = isDarkBand ? "light" : "sage";
const secondaryVariant = isDarkBand ? "ghost-light" : "ghost";
---

<section class:list={["section", bandClass]} id={id} data-vs-reveal-batch>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class:list={[`${fam}-grid`, ratioClass]}>
      {
        !mediaRight && hasMedia && (
          <div class="what-img" data-vs-parallax data-parallax="-25">
            <Image
              src={src}
              width={width}
              height={height}
              alt={alt}
              widths={[500, 800, 1100]}
              sizes="(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 50vw"
            />
          </div>
        )
      }

      <div class={`${fam}-text`}>
        {eyebrow && <span class:list={["eyebrow", eyebrowLight && "light"]}>{eyebrow}</span>}
        {
          heading && (
            <h2>
              <Fragment set:html={heading} />
            </h2>
          )
        }
        {/* `.natural-quote` whichever side the photo is on. The rule is the
            family's only pull-quote treatment and no `.what-quote` has ever
            existed, so naming it after the side it happens to appear on today
            would invent a second class for one appearance. */}
        {quote && <div class="natural-quote">{quote}</div>}{/* `}{` with nothing
            between, on the same rule the callout and the second button below
            follow: an expression on its own line carries a newline-and-indent
            TEXT NODE either side, and that survives into the HTML as a rendered
            space even when the condition is false. Every band saved before today
            has an empty checklist, so a line of its own here would widen the gap
            between the quote and the prose on every one of them — a diff on
            pages that gained nothing. Flush against the previous brace, this
            adds no text node at all, and the single whitespace node that already
            sat between the quote and the prose is still the only one there. The
            comment lives INSIDE the expression because a comment node on its own
            line would reintroduce exactly what it documents.

            AFTER THE QUOTE AND BEFORE THE PROSE, which is where all four pages
            that ship this list put it: `.natural-text` is eyebrow, h2,
            `.natural-quote`, `ul.candidate-list`, and none of the four has a
            prose column in the same band. Both orderings reproduce those four
            identically; this one is the order the field sits in on the layout
            (quote, then checklist, then the buttons) and the order the markup
            reads in today. */
          hasChecklist && (
            <ul class="candidate-list">
              {checklistPoints.map((point, i) => (
                <li>
                  <span class="marker">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
                  <span>{point.lead === "" ? point.item : <Fragment><b>{point.lead}</b> {point.item}</Fragment>}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          )
        }
        {/* `.prose` appears only if it would hold at least one paragraph — an
            empty `body_2` on a band that has no `body` either must not leave a
            bare `<div class="prose">` behind. */}
        {
          (body || hasBody2) && (
            <div class="prose">
              {body && <p>{body}</p>}
              {hasBody2 &&
                (body2IsBlock ? (
                  <Fragment set:html={body2Html} />
                ) : (
                  <p set:html={body2Html} />
                ))}
            </div>
          )
        }{/* `}{` with nothing between, deliberately. An expression on its own
            line carries a newline-plus-indent TEXT NODE either side, and that
            survives into the HTML as a rendered space even when the condition is
            false — the harness caught it as `</a> </div>` becoming
            `</a>  </div>` on a page with no aside at all. Sitting flush against
            the previous closing brace leaves exactly one whitespace node between
            the prose and the CTA row, which is what was there before this field
            existed. A comment node here would reintroduce the problem it
            documents, so this comment lives INSIDE the same expression. */
          hasCallout && !hasSubCards && (
            <aside class="veneers-callout">
              {calloutHeadingText && <h3>{calloutHeadingText}</h3>}
              {calloutBodyIsBlock ? (
                <Fragment set:html={calloutBodyHtml} />
              ) : (
                calloutParagraphs.map((para) => <p set:html={para} />)
              )}
            </aside>
          )
        }
        {
          hasCtaRow && (
            <div class="cta-row left">
              <div class="cta-buttons">
                {hasCta && (
                  <Button
                    variant={primaryVariant}
                    href={ctaHref ?? "#"}
                    hoverLabel={CTA_HOVER[ctaHref ?? ""]}
                  >
                    {ctaLabel}
                  </Button>
                )}{/* flush against the previous brace: an expression on its own
                     line carries a whitespace text node either side, which
                     renders as a space even when the condition is false. */
                  hasCta2 && (
                  <Button
                    variant={secondaryVariant}
                    href={ctaHref2 ?? "#"}
                    hoverLabel={CTA_HOVER[ctaHref2 ?? ""]}
                  >
                    {ctaLabel2}
                  </Button>
                )}
              </div>
            </div>
          )
        }
      </div>

      {
        mediaRight && hasMedia && (
          <div class="natural-img" data-vs-parallax data-parallax="-25">
            <Image
              src={src}
              width={width}
              height={height}
              alt={alt}
              widths={[500, 800, 1100]}
              sizes="(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 50vw"
            />
          </div>
        )
      }
    </div>{/* `}{`-flush against the `.what-grid` close, on the rule the quote,
        the callout and the second button in this file already follow: an
        expression on its own line carries a newline-and-indent TEXT NODE either
        side, and that survives into the HTML as a rendered space even when the
        condition is false. Eleven bands are already saved against this layout
        with no `sub_cards`, so a line of its own here would add whitespace to
        the markup of every one of them — a diff on pages that gained nothing.
        Flush against the tag, a false condition emits exactly the bytes that
        were here before, and the single whitespace node that already sat between
        `.what-grid` and the close of `.wrap` is still the only one there. The
        comment lives INSIDE the expression because a comment node on its own
        line would reintroduce precisely what it documents.

        AFTER `.what-grid` AND INSIDE `.wrap`, which is where both pages put it
        (all-on-4-single-arch.astro:413, sinus-lift.astro:380). Not inside
        `.what-text`: `.what-sub` is a full-width centred unit, and nesting it in
        the copy column would render it at half width against the photo.

        THE WRAPPER IS INSIDE THE TEST, NOT AROUND IT — `.what-sub` carries
        `margin-top: 70px`, so an empty one is 70px of visible gap at the foot of
        every band that has no cards. */
      hasSubCards && (
        <div class="what-sub">
          {calloutIsSubHead && (
            <div class="what-sub-head">
              {calloutHeadingText && <h3>{calloutHeadingText}</h3>}
              {/* The `.prose` wrapper is in the markup being reproduced here
                  (`.what-sub-head > .prose > p`) and is NOT in the aside above,
                  which puts its paragraphs directly in the `<aside>`. Same two
                  branches as the aside otherwise: a pre-wrapped value passes
                  through untouched, because `<p><p>…</p></p>` is invalid nesting
                  that the parser closes early and leaves a stray empty `<p>`
                  behind. The blank-line split is the aside's, for the same
                  reason — splitting on a bare newline would turn every soft wrap
                  in the textarea into a paragraph break. */}
              {calloutBodyIsBlock ? (
                <div class="prose" set:html={calloutBodyHtml} />
              ) : (
                calloutParagraphs.length > 0 && (
                  <div class="prose">
                    {calloutParagraphs.map((para) => (
                      <p set:html={para} />
                    ))}
                  </div>
                )
              )}
            </div>
          )}
          {/* The eyebrow above that `<h3>` — "Upper vs. Lower" on all-on-4,
              "Common Causes" on sinus-lift — has NO field on this layout and is
              a recorded known gap, so nothing is emitted for it. An empty
              `<span class="eyebrow">` would be worse than nothing rather than
              harmless: the head's eyebrow rule hangs a 28px hairline off
              `::before` AND `::after`, so a label-less span renders as two
              floating rules with a hole between them. */}
          <div class:list={["config-grid", `cols-${subColumnsKey}`]}>
            {subCardRows.map((card) => (
              <article class="config-card">
                {card.tag && <span class="tag">{card.tag}</span>}
                {card.title && <h4>{card.title}</h4>}
                {card.body && <p>{card.body}</p>}
              </article>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      )
    }
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* ---------- band shell ----------
     `.section` owns its padding and has no margin at all, so this band cannot
     collapse into whichever band an editor drags above or below it. Same rule
     as FinalBand.astro. */
  .section {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 110px 0;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
  }
  .section.cream {
    background: var(--vs-cream);
  }
  .section.dark {
    background: var(--vs-sage-pale);
  }
  .section.sage {
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  .section.alt {
    background: var(--vs-charcoal-green);
  }

  .wrap {
    max-width: 1240px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 40px;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .eyebrow.light {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  /* The eyebrow's single rule is a leading hairline, not the head band's pair —
     this layout's copy column is left-aligned, so a trailing rule would run into
     the gutter. */
  .what-text .eyebrow,
  .natural-text .eyebrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  .what-text .eyebrow::before,
  .natural-text .eyebrow::before {
    content: "";
    width: 28px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }

  /* h2 resolves to ink. The page sheet reaches that colour only through a
     trailing override block whose comment says it works because of its file
     position; a block that owns its cascade states the outcome once. */
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em;
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.2vw, 54px);
  }
  /* `:global()` because the `<em>` arrives through `set:html` and carries no
     scope attribute — a plain `h2 em` compiles to `em[data-astro-cid]`, misses
     it, and drops the accent colour from every heading on the site. */
  h2 :global(em) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--vs-sage-soft);
    font-weight: 300;
  }

  p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }

  /* The two families' headings differ by 2px of bottom margin. Preserved rather
     than levelled: this file's job is to reproduce, and a 2px change is exactly
     the size of thing a screenshot diff flags and nobody can explain a year
     later. */
  .what-text h2 {
    margin: 22px 0 26px;
    max-width: 14ch;
  }
  .natural-text h2 {
    margin: 22px 0 28px;
    max-width: 14ch;
  }

  .prose p {
    font-size: 16.5px;
    line-height: 1.85;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .prose p + p {
    margin-top: 18px;
  }
  /* The pre-wrapped `body_2` path only: those paragraphs arrive through
     `set:html` and carry no scope attribute, so `.prose p` above compiles past
     them. Same typography, stated once more for the elements the scoping
     cannot reach. The scoped rule is more specific and still wins where both
     apply, and the declarations are identical, so nothing shifts. */
  .prose :global(p) {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
    font-size: 16.5px;
    line-height: 1.85;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .prose :global(p + p) {
    margin-top: 18px;
  }

  /* ---------- band inversions ----------
     Written band-wide here, unlike the head-scoped inversions in FaqBlock and
     CardGridBlock: this layout has no card sitting on its own surface, so every
     piece of type in it genuinely does sit on the band and genuinely does have
     to invert. The pilot's two media splits are both charcoal, and this is the
     rule set that keeps them white-on-charcoal. */
  .section.alt h2,
  .section.sage h2 {
    color: #fff;
  }
  .section.alt h2 :global(em),
  .section.sage h2 :global(em) {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  .section.alt p,
  .section.sage p {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  }
  .section.alt .eyebrow,
  .section.sage .eyebrow {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  .section.alt .eyebrow::before,
  .section.sage .eyebrow::before {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
  }
  .section.alt .natural-quote,
  .section.sage .natural-quote {
    color: #fff;
    border-top-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.25);
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.25);
  }
  /* `body_2`'s inline link on the two dark bands.
     global.css DOES invert `.vs-link` on charcoal, but it reaches it through a
     hand-maintained `:is(.caligners .section.alt, .pveneers .section.alt, …)`
     list of PAGE-SCOPE classes. A band rendered from the CMS has no page class
     to match, so a link inside a charcoal media_split would keep the default
     ink and vanish into the background. Stated here so the band carries its own
     answer instead of waiting to be added to a list in another file.
     `:global()` because the anchor arrives through `set:html` and carries no
     scope attribute; the compound is more specific than global.css's rule and
     resolves to the same colour, so the page-scoped pages are unaffected. */
  .section.alt .prose :global(.vs-link),
  .section.sage .prose :global(.vs-link) {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
    text-decoration-color: rgba(232, 234, 216, 0.5);
  }
  .section.alt .prose :global(.vs-link:hover),
  .section.alt .prose :global(.vs-link:focus-visible),
  .section.sage .prose :global(.vs-link:hover),
  .section.sage .prose :global(.vs-link:focus-visible) {
    color: #fff;
    text-decoration-color: #fff;
  }

  /* The checklist on the two dark bands.
     These three declarations are not a design choice made here — they are the
     values the four page sheets already ship, because all four places a
     `.candidate-list` appears in the corpus are on `.section.alt`
     (`.bgraft`, `.aos4`, `.fmdi`, and `.sinuslift`'s identically-declared
     `.tech-list`). They are stated in this block rather than left to the page
     sheet for the reason `.vs-link` above documents at length: every one of
     those rules is namespaced under a PAGE class, and a band rendered from the
     CMS has no page class to match, so a migrated checklist on a charcoal band
     would fall through to the light-band colours below and print near-black
     text on charcoal.
     Placed with the other band inversions, and more specific than the base
     rules further down, so source order between the two sections is irrelevant
     — same arrangement as `.natural-quote`. */
  .section.alt .candidate-list .marker,
  .section.sage .candidate-list .marker {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  .section.alt .candidate-list b,
  .section.sage .candidate-list b {
    color: #fff;
  }
  .section.alt .candidate-list span,
  .section.sage .candidate-list span {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  }

  .section.dark h2 {
    color: var(--vs-ink);
  }
  .section.dark h2 :global(em) {
    color: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  .section.dark p,
  .section.dark .eyebrow {
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }
  .section.dark .eyebrow::before {
    background: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }

  /* ---------- the split ----------
     Photo left: even columns, landscape plate. Photo right: the copy gets the
     narrower track and the plate is a portrait. See the header for why these
     travel together. */
  .what-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 80px;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .natural-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.1fr;
    gap: 80px;
    align-items: center;
  }

  /* A modifier only ever fires when the editor asked for a split the family
     does not already have — `.what-grid.ratio-even` and
     `.natural-grid.ratio-wide-media` are never emitted. The tracks are written
     photo-relative, so `wide-media` widens whichever track holds the photo. */
  .what-grid.ratio-wide-media {
    grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 1fr;
  }
  .what-grid.ratio-wide-text {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.1fr;
  }
  .natural-grid.ratio-even {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  }
  .natural-grid.ratio-wide-text {
    grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 1fr;
  }

  .what-img {
    aspect-ratio: 5/4;
    border-radius: var(--vs-r-xl);
    overflow: hidden;
    position: relative;
    box-shadow: var(--vs-shadow);
  }
  .what-img img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
    transition: transform 0.6s ease;
  }
  .what-img:hover img {
    transform: scale(1.03);
  }

  .natural-img {
    aspect-ratio: 4/5;
    border-radius: var(--vs-r-xl);
    overflow: hidden;
    box-shadow: var(--vs-shadow);
  }
  .natural-img img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    transition: transform 0.6s ease;
  }
  .natural-img:hover img {
    transform: scale(1.03);
  }

  .natural-quote {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: clamp(22px, 2.2vw, 28px);
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    line-height: 1.4;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    padding: 24px 0 26px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    margin-bottom: 26px;
    max-width: 36ch;
  }

  /* ---------- checklist ----------
     `auto 1fr` with `align-items: baseline`, so the italic numeral sits on the
     first BASELINE of its line's text rather than the top of the box — a marker
     that is 20px display italic next to 15px body would otherwise ride high on
     every row, and the misalignment only shows once a line wraps.

     `.candidate-list b` gets `margin-right: 6px` AND the markup keeps a literal
     space after `</b>`. Both are in the pages this reproduces, and the space is
     load-bearing on its own: `b` is `inline-block`, so a browser that had only
     the margin would still break the line between the dash and the first word
     with no space to show for it.

     The colours here are the LIGHT-band case, which no page in the corpus
     exercises today — all four draw this list on charcoal. They are derived from
     what this block already does to its own type: the marker takes the quote's
     sage, `b` takes the h2's ink, the line takes a paragraph's ink-soft. The
     charcoal and sage bands override all three above. */
  .candidate-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 18px;
  }
  .candidate-list li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    gap: 14px;
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .candidate-list .marker {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 300;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .candidate-list b {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    font-size: 16.5px;
    display: inline-block;
    margin-right: 6px;
  }
  /* Less specific than `.marker` above, which is why the numeral keeps its own
     size and colour despite also being a span. Same arrangement, same order, as
     the four page sheets. */
  .candidate-list span {
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }
  /* The list ships `margin: 0` on all four pages because on all four it is the
     last thing in the column — the quote's own 26px bottom margin makes the gap
     above it, and there is nothing below. A band that filled BOTH the checklist
     and the copy would butt the first paragraph straight against the last line
     of the list. Stated on the combinator so it can only ever fire when both are
     present, which is never on the four pages this reproduces; 26px is the
     quote's bottom margin, i.e. the rhythm this column already has. */
  .candidate-list + .prose {
    margin-top: 26px;
  }

  /* ---------- the secondary card grid ----------
     `.what-sub` sits after the split inside the same `.wrap`, and it is the same
     unit on both pages that ship it. Every declaration below is copied from a
     page sheet and names the sheet and line it came from.

     SCOPING BUYS NO SPECIFICITY HERE, and every selector below is written on the
     assumption that it does not. Astro compiles a scoped rule to
     `.what-sub:where(.astro-…) .config-card:where(.astro-…)`, and `:where()` is
     specificity ZERO by definition — so that selector is (0,2,0) flat, exactly
     what `.aos4 .config-card` is. A tie is resolved by whichever sheet the
     bundler emits last, which is not a thing to bet a live band on. So each rule
     here is qualified until it OUTRANKS the page rule it has to survive, rather
     than merely matching it: `.what-sub` prefixes the head rules that would
     otherwise tie with the page's `.aos4 h3`, and the band inversions further
     down carry the full `.section.alt …` compound. Two defects on this project
     came from assuming the page sheet no longer had a say.

     The wrapper keeps the name `.what-sub` on BOTH families. It is a sibling of
     the split rather than a part of it, and no `.natural-sub` exists anywhere in
     the corpus to reproduce, so deriving the name from `fam` would invent a
     second class for one shape.

     all-on-4-single-arch.css:342-344 and sinus-lift.css:366-368 — identical. */
  .what-sub {
    margin-top: 70px;
  }
  /* all-on-4-single-arch.css:346-350 / sinus-lift.css:369-372 — identical. */
  .what-sub .what-sub-head {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 32px;
  }
  /* all-on-4-single-arch.css:361-366 / sinus-lift.css:380-384 — identical.
     `margin` and `line-height` are NOT in those blocks: they come from the
     page-wide reset at all-on-4-single-arch.css:39-42
     (`.aos4 h1, .aos4 h2, .aos4 h3, .aos4 h4 { margin: 0; line-height: 1.1 }`),
     which no block inherits and global.css does not supply — it resets `p` and
     `body` only. Omitted, the `<h3>` would take the UA's 1em margins and 1.15
     line-height and the head would read visibly looser than the page's.

     The `.what-sub` prefix on this rule and the two around it is not decoration:
     without it the selector is (0,1,1), a dead tie with the page-wide
     `.aos4 h3 { font-size: clamp(20px, 2vw, 26px); color: var(--ink) }` at
     all-on-4-single-arch.css:47, and the head would take the page's size or this
     block's depending on emit order. */
  .what-sub .what-sub-head h3 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    font-size: clamp(24px, 2.8vw, 34px);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin: 14px 0 0;
  }
  /* all-on-4-single-arch.css:367-370 / sinus-lift.css:385-386 — identical.
     `margin-inline: auto` is what actually centres the paragraph: this block's
     own `.prose p` caps it at 70ch, and a capped block in a `text-align: center`
     column still sits hard against the left edge without it. The `:global()`
     twin is for the pre-wrapped `body`/`callout_body` path, whose paragraphs
     arrive through `set:html` carrying no scope attribute — the same reason
     `.prose :global(p)` exists further up. */
  .what-sub .what-sub-head .prose {
    margin-top: 14px;
  }
  .what-sub .what-sub-head .prose p,
  .what-sub .what-sub-head .prose :global(p) {
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  /* The sub-head's `<h3>` on the inverted bands. The base sage above is what the
     two page sheets declare, but it is NOT what either page renders: both `#what`
     bands are `.section.alt`, and `.aos4 .section.alt h3 { color: #fff }`
     (all-on-4-single-arch.css:228-231) outranks `.aos4 .what-sub-head h3` at
     (0,3,1) against (0,2,1). Reproducing the sheet without reproducing the
     cascade that overrides it would put sage-on-charcoal where the live page has
     white. `.section.dark` follows all-on-4-single-arch.css:238-241 the same
     way. The base rule is left as sage because that IS the light-band answer,
     which no page in the corpus exercises yet. */
  .section.alt .what-sub .what-sub-head h3,
  .section.sage .what-sub .what-sub-head h3 {
    color: #fff;
  }
  .section.dark .what-sub .what-sub-head h3 {
    color: var(--vs-ink);
  }

  /* Track counts, from the two sheets that ship this grid:
       2 — `.aos4 .config-grid`     (all-on-4-single-arch.css:371-373)
       4 — `.sinuslift .cause-grid` (sinus-lift.css:387-389)
     3 has no `sub_cards` band in the corpus today, but the select offers it, so
     it is taken from `.aos4 .alternatives-grid`
     (all-on-4-single-arch.css:530-531) — the only three-across grid in this
     family. The gutter follows the track count exactly as the sheets do: 24px at
     two across, 18px at four. */
  .what-sub .config-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 24px;
  }
  .what-sub .config-grid.cols-2 {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  }
  .what-sub .config-grid.cols-3 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  }
  .what-sub .config-grid.cols-4 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 18px;
  }
  /* The four-across drops to two before 1240px and to one at 780px
     (sinus-lift.css:875-877 and :923). Two and three across drop straight to one
     at 1100px, which is where `.aos4 .config-grid` and `.aos4 .alternatives-grid`
     both go (all-on-4-single-arch.css:824-825) and also where this block's own
     split stacks. Kept beside the rules they modify rather than in the trailing
